1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,284
Multiply a plus b by itself,
and you get three terms.

2
00:00:04,684 --> 00:00:07,768
Multiply it three times,
and you get four terms.

3
00:00:07,768 --> 00:00:09,455
It already takes a while.

4
00:00:09,905 --> 00:00:13,576
Aarav's homework tonight asks
for a plus b, to the power ten.

5
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:15,896
Ten brackets, multiplied one by one.

6
00:00:15,896 --> 00:00:17,560
That is a whole page of work.

7
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:18,729
Is there a shortcut?

8
00:00:19,229 --> 00:00:20,129
There is.

9
00:00:19,949 --> 00:00:21,888
One line gives the whole answer.

10
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:23,915
It is called the binomial theorem.

11
00:00:24,565 --> 00:00:26,197
Start at the canteen.

12
00:00:26,197 --> 00:00:29,790
Three friends each order
one drink, tea or coffee.

13
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:34,498
Each friend has two choices, so there
are two times two times two, eight

14
00:00:34,498 --> 00:00:35,619
different orders.

15
00:00:36,019 --> 00:00:38,428
In maths, tea is a and coffee is b.

16
00:00:38,428 --> 00:00:40,161
Each friend is one bracket.

17
00:00:40,161 --> 00:00:43,254
A plus b, cubed, is just
three friends choosing.

18
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:45,845
Now watch all eight orders appear.

19
00:00:45,845 --> 00:00:47,750
Tea, tea, tea gives a cubed.

20
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,008
Tea, tea, coffee gives a squared b.

21
00:00:50,408 --> 00:00:52,984
Now sort them by how
many coffees there are.

22
00:00:53,384 --> 00:00:55,007
No coffee: one order.

23
00:00:55,007 --> 00:00:56,828
One coffee: three orders.

24
00:00:56,828 --> 00:00:58,698
Two coffees: three orders.

25
00:00:58,698 --> 00:01:00,518
Three coffees: one order.

26
00:01:01,168 --> 00:01:06,514
So a plus b, cubed, is a cubed, plus
three a squared b, plus three a b

27
00:01:06,514 --> 00:01:08,331
squared, plus b cubed.

28
00:01:08,981 --> 00:01:10,643
Here is the key question.

29
00:01:10,643 --> 00:01:13,728
Why are there exactly three
orders with one coffee?

30
00:01:14,128 --> 00:01:16,836
Because you only decide
who gets the coffee.

31
00:01:16,836 --> 00:01:19,683
The first friend, the
second, or the third.

32
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:24,243
Choosing r things out of n is n C
r, from the combinations episode.

33
00:01:24,243 --> 00:01:26,130
Here, three C one is three.

34
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:29,824
So every number in front
of a term is an n C r.

35
00:01:29,824 --> 00:01:32,786
It counts the ways to choose
which brackets give b.

36
00:01:33,436 --> 00:01:35,701
Now let the number of friends grow.

37
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:37,928
Watch n count up from one to ten.

38
00:01:38,328 --> 00:01:40,682
Each new power adds one more term.

39
00:01:40,682 --> 00:01:45,181
The numbers grow fast, and they
rise and fall evenly, like a hill.

40
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:49,964
At n equals ten, the numbers add
up to one thousand and twenty four.

41
00:01:49,964 --> 00:01:54,347
That is two to the ten, because
each of ten friends had two choices.

42
00:01:54,997 --> 00:01:57,524
Now we can write the theorem in one line.

43
00:01:57,874 --> 00:01:58,774
Step one.

44
00:01:58,649 --> 00:02:00,710
The first term is a to the n.

45
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:03,253
Nobody picks b, and n C zero is one.

46
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:04,903
Step two.

47
00:02:04,778 --> 00:02:07,459
In each next term, one a becomes one b.

48
00:02:07,459 --> 00:02:11,915
The power of a goes down by one,
and the power of b goes up by one.

49
00:02:12,665 --> 00:02:13,565
Step three.

50
00:02:13,535 --> 00:02:17,869
In front of a to the n minus r,
times b to the r, sits n C r.

51
00:02:17,869 --> 00:02:21,341
That is the number of ways
to choose r brackets for b.

52
00:02:22,091 --> 00:02:22,991
Step four.

53
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:27,195
The last term is b to the n,
with n C n, which is also one.

54
00:02:27,945 --> 00:02:29,975
So there are n plus one terms.

55
00:02:29,975 --> 00:02:33,071
And in every term, the
two powers add up to n.

56
00:02:33,721 --> 00:02:34,787
Side by side.

57
00:02:34,787 --> 00:02:39,511
Many people think a plus b, squared,
is just a squared plus b squared.

58
00:02:39,911 --> 00:02:42,266
Look at a square with side a plus b.

59
00:02:42,266 --> 00:02:43,994
It splits into four pieces.

60
00:02:43,994 --> 00:02:47,686
a squared, b squared, and two
rectangles, each a times b.

61
00:02:48,086 --> 00:02:52,667
So a plus b, squared, is a squared,
plus two a b, plus b squared.

62
00:02:52,667 --> 00:02:55,511
The middle term is what
the shortcut forgets.

63
00:02:56,111 --> 00:02:57,139
Now you try.

64
00:02:57,139 --> 00:03:00,800
Is a plus b, cubed, equal
to a cubed plus b cubed?

65
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,077
Pause and decide.

66
00:03:05,277 --> 00:03:06,177
No!

67
00:03:05,707 --> 00:03:10,241
The middle terms, three a squared b
and three a b squared, are missing.

68
00:03:10,241 --> 00:03:12,555
Only the first and last terms match.

69
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:14,234
Remember this.

70
00:03:14,234 --> 00:03:18,205
Pick one part from every bracket,
and n C r counts the ways.

71
00:03:18,905 --> 00:03:20,595
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:20,945 --> 00:03:23,615
Expand x plus two, to the power four.

73
00:03:24,115 --> 00:03:25,015
Step one.

74
00:03:24,899 --> 00:03:28,967
n is four, so the numbers are
four C zero up to four C four.

75
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:31,120
One, four, six, four, one.

76
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:32,770
Step two.

77
00:03:32,655 --> 00:03:35,478
The powers of x go down from four to zero.

78
00:03:35,478 --> 00:03:38,301
The powers of two go up from zero to four.

79
00:03:39,051 --> 00:03:39,987
Step three.

80
00:03:39,987 --> 00:03:41,492
Multiply each column.

81
00:03:41,492 --> 00:03:42,702
x to the four.

82
00:03:42,702 --> 00:03:43,851
Eight x cubed.

83
00:03:43,851 --> 00:03:45,468
Twenty four x squared.

84
00:03:45,468 --> 00:03:46,567
Thirty two x.

85
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:47,467
Sixteen.

86
00:03:48,039 --> 00:03:49,888
Check with x equal to one.

87
00:03:49,888 --> 00:03:54,437
Three to the four is eighty one, and
one plus eight plus twenty four plus

88
00:03:54,437 --> 00:03:57,224
thirty two plus sixteen
is also eighty one.

89
00:03:57,874 --> 00:03:59,338
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:59,688 --> 00:04:02,374
Expand x minus two, to the power four.

91
00:04:02,374 --> 00:04:05,207
Many students turn
every plus into a minus.

92
00:04:05,607 --> 00:04:08,677
But only odd powers of
minus two are negative.

93
00:04:08,677 --> 00:04:11,121
Minus two, squared, is plus four.

94
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:15,134
So the signs alternate: plus,
minus, plus, minus, plus.

95
00:04:15,784 --> 00:04:16,902
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:16,902 --> 00:04:20,383
In JEE Main, as questions on the sum
of all the coefficients, which you

97
00:04:20,383 --> 00:04:22,010
get by putting x equal to one.

98
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:23,825
Exam level now.

99
00:04:23,825 --> 00:04:26,574
How many terms does this expansion have?

100
00:04:26,574 --> 00:04:27,840
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:34,826
The answer is B.

102
00:04:34,826 --> 00:04:35,726
Nine.

103
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:37,419
Put x equal to one.

104
00:04:37,419 --> 00:04:41,043
The coefficients add up to
one plus two, to the power n.

105
00:04:41,043 --> 00:04:42,691
That is three to the n.

106
00:04:43,191 --> 00:04:47,967
Three to the eight is exactly six
thousand five hundred sixty one, so n

107
00:04:47,967 --> 00:04:48,867
is eight.

108
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:52,308
There is always one more
term than the power.

109
00:04:52,308 --> 00:04:55,228
So the expansion has
nine terms, not eight.

110
00:04:55,828 --> 00:04:57,368
Here is your recap.

111
00:04:57,368 --> 00:04:58,951
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:02,151 --> 00:05:04,337
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:04,737 --> 00:05:05,637
One.

114
00:05:05,199 --> 00:05:09,032
Why is a plus b, squared, not
just a squared plus b squared?

115
00:05:09,932 --> 00:05:10,832
Two.

116
00:05:10,399 --> 00:05:13,984
How many terms does a plus
b, to the power seven, have?

117
00:05:14,884 --> 00:05:15,784
Three.

118
00:05:15,391 --> 00:05:19,378
What do the coefficients of one plus
x, to the power five, add up to?

119
00:05:20,478 --> 00:05:24,293
Next time: you need only the
seventh term of a long expansion.

120
00:05:24,293 --> 00:05:26,132
Can you jump straight to it?

121
00:05:26,132 --> 00:05:28,301
We find out with the general term.
